I deliberately left staking/tokenomics out of the core protocol. You can add an on-chain usage/payment layer later without contaminating the thing we're actually trying to prove works.

DeepProve fits very precisely

Lagrange's current DeepProve repository now explicitly supports end-to-end LLM proofs for GPT-2, Gemma 3 and Llama 2; their public numbers for 512-token proofs are still measured in minutes on a large CPU machine, so it should be an optional certification path rather than the inference hot loop.

The useful certificate is therefore three-layered:

1. COMMITMENT

this exact:
world + Pack + context + output + evaluator

2. INFERENCE PROOF

supported model M
really ran on context C
and produced output O

3. CAPABILITY CERTIFICATE

across N hidden worlds:
M + Pack P
performed Δ better than M alone

DeepProve can address #2. It cannot cryptographically prove #3 by itself. Lagrange describes the primitive as proving that an output came from executing the relevant model/input computation.

And importantly, closed proprietary API models generally cannot receive this kind of zkML model proof unless their provider exposes compatible weights or another attestation mechanism.

So you could eventually advertise:

SOCIAL-GAME-THEORY PACK 41

model: Llama-X
pack: 0x81af...

hidden Cogym suite:
baseline                62.1
pack                    77.4
Δ                      +15.3

herding susceptibility -28%
revision quality       +19%
calibration            +11%

trials                   400

model inference:
DeepProve ✓
pack commitment ✓
benchmark commitment ✓

That's much more interesting than a prompt marketplace.

The “school → graduate” analogy is almost literal

A specialist agent can progress:

YEAR 0
generic model

YEAR 1
basic domain state recognition

YEAR 2
reasoning procedures

YEAR 3
adversarial / failure scenarios

YEAR 4
social / multi-agent scenarios

YEAR 5
non-stationary hard worlds

GRADUATION
passes hidden suite

But there's another path:

generic model
     +
Graduate Pack
     ↓
attempt to reproduce
graduate behavioral basin

So a Pack is almost a compressed education.

ReasoningBank strongly supports the experience side of this: it explicitly distills reusable strategies from successful and failed trajectories, then retrieves those lessons for later tasks.

MemEvolve then makes the next recursive step possible: eventually the school's encode/store/retrieve/manage strategy can itself evolve.

BEAM/LIGHT also gives us a natural curriculum ablation:

full raw history
vs
episodic retrieval
vs
working memory
vs
compressed scratchpad
vs
Cogym Pack

LIGHT's three-memory architecture improves long-horizon memory across its evaluated models, while BEAM demonstrates that simply throwing enormous context windows at the problem doesn't eliminate degradation.

Loom is useful, but I did not depend on it

You were right about ghuntley's Loom being architecturally relevant.

It has exactly the engineering properties we'd want: Rust core abstractions, LLM providers behind interfaces, a tool registry, persisted conversation threads, server-side LLM proxying, structured analytics, runtime experiment flags, and reproducible builds.

But its own README explicitly says it is experimental/unstable and its code is proprietary.

So v0.2 steals the pattern:

CORE
stable protocols

ADAPTERS
Hydra
LLM providers
DeepProve

CLI
thin interface

not Loom itself.

